---
/**
 * A small turning ring, for a page that is only ever passing through and has
 * nothing of its own to show while it does (see /work).
 *
 * The class is `spinner-ring` and not `spinner` because global.css already
 * owns that name, for the image viewer's indicator, which ships `display:
 * none` until the lightbox turns it on. Scoping does not save a component
 * from that: the scoped rule outranks the global one only on the properties
 * it declares, and `display` is not one of them, so a ring called `spinner`
 * inherits the `none` and never paints.
 *
 * Decorative, so it is hidden from assistive tech: the text beside it is
 * what says the page is going somewhere.
 */
---

<div class="spinner-ring" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<style>
  /* Themed rather than fixed. The ring sits on the card's well, which is
     near-white in light and near-black in dark, so one hard-coded grey would
     be invisible against one of the two. */
  .spinner-ring {
    width: 24px;
    height: 24px;
    border: 3px solid var(--color-chip-border);
    border-top-color: var(--color-text-muted);
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  /* Guarded like every other animation here. Stood still the ring still
     reads as a waiting mark, and the gap it fills is a blink either way. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .spinner-ring {
      /* Astro scopes selectors but leaves @keyframes names global, so this
         one is namespaced by hand -- a plain `spin` is a name anything else
         could claim, and the last one parsed would win for everybody. */
      animation: spinner-ring-turn 850ms ease-in-out infinite;
    }
  }

  @keyframes spinner-ring-turn {
    to {
      transform: rotate(1turn);
    }
  }
</style>
